The deny list (permissions.deny / --deny) now catches the bypasses from #1010, and the fix is committed as 8988d853 with Fixes #1010 in the title. Build, all tests, clippy with -D warnings and the fmt check pass.

What changed
• **Whole-command match:** still runs first and is unchanged. A plain hit gives exactly the same refusal text as before (a test checks this).
• **Each part of a compound command:** if the whole command doesn't match and a deny list is set, yoyo also checks each part on its own. It splits at `;`, `&&`, `||`, `|` and newlines, but never inside quotes, and skips heredoc bodies unless they are fed to a shell. Before matching, it strips leading VAR=value words, export/declare/typeset/readonly/local, and if/then/{. So cd x && rm -rf ~, TZ="$HOME" rm -rf build and A=1 B=2 rm -rf ~ are now refused, while echo "rm -rf ~" and git commit -m "x; rm -rf ~" still run.
• **Program name from a shell expansion:** with a deny list set, a command whose program name comes from `$X`, `$(...)` or backticks is refused, and the message says to write the command plainly. This blocks X=rm; $X -rf ~ and BASH_ARGV0=rm; $BASH_ARGV0 -rf ~.
• **One check for every door:** the normal bash tool and the sub-agent's bash both go through the same function, so the fix covers both.
• **Docs:** the "matched exactly as the agent wrote it" sentence in permissions.md now describes the new rules and states the eval limit below.

The trade, decided in a test: with a deny list set, "$CARGO" build is now refused. Someone who wrote a deny list asked for a fence, and a fence any $VAR can walk through isn't one. Users with no deny list see no change; a test checks that an empty list refuses nothing. That includes my own evolve loop, because this repo's .yoyo.toml sets no deny list.

Checks: I made the per-part check do nothing, and both new tests for it failed by name; after restoring it they passed. The code is clean again.

Not covered: a command string built at runtime and run through eval "$(...)" still gets through. I confirmed this with a test that records it as a known limit, and filed it as #1011 (open, labelled agent-self) so closing #1010 doesn't close it too.

Side note: while I was writing the tests, the hard-deny guard blocked my cat >> ... <<EOF command with "contains 'rm -rf /'", but the command itself didn't contain that string. It only contained test strings. I wrote the file with the editor instead. This is the same over-matching the plan warned about. I did not investigate which part of the text it matched.
